it's commendable to create a compelling  video game world it's even more  impressive if that world has its own  history but it is downright astonishing  when that history actually effects how  we play a game and after 45 hours with  Ghost Recon breakpoint I can confidently  say this is one of those worlds the  South Pacific archipelago or OA is  saturated with personality and secrets  it's filled with remnants of a fictional  past and nods to an uncertain future in  a game with glaring systemic issues and  a poor scripted narrative I still can't  help but marvel at the way breakpoints  century spanning story is intertwine  with its gameplay and despite my  frustrations I'm obsessed with every  detail yes breakpoint is undoubtedly a  departure from the series roots but it  actually nails the idea of recon more  than some of its predecessors this is  due in large part to its intricate world  provided you play with exploration mode  enabled the majority of your time will  be spent gathering Intel scouting new  locations and mapping the archipelago  not only does this slow burn exploration  reinforce the sense that you're being  hunted in an unfamiliar land  it also helps add variety to your  playthrough my mo and wild lands was to  clear every outpost that came across but  in break point I'm conditioned to  prioritize information above anything  else I'm satisfied to enter a base  eliminate only immediate threats and  leave once I have new Intel in a sense  I'm dictating my own fun as I adapt to  the surroundings and it's this world's  backstory that helps me flesh out that  fun while I'm hacking computers stealing  documents helping civilians and  interrogating soldiers I'm also peeling  back numerous layers of environmental  storytelling on a massive scale the more  Explorer or OA the more I become  entangled in its fictional past so the  way I see it or OA comprises five  distinct let's call them chronological  strata to use an overly complicated term  first off is the islands immediate  present a state-of-the-art settlement  built by the tech giants tell in an  effort to create a utopia it's an  architectural portmanteau of brutalist  design and surveil estate machinery the  sleek lines of these town  more than just aesthetics though every  time I see a skel city I know to prepare  for urban combat often against advance  drones or well outfitted soldiers what's  more each time I find a skel railway or  supply road I know that it will  inevitably lead somewhere  often these locations contain useful  items crucial Intel or sometimes they're  just making the world feel more lived-in  like a winery or a brewery in fact I  have yet to find a scale structure that  doesn't serve some diegetic purpose in  the game world  our OS next layer of environmental  storytelling brings us back to the 20th  century along with its fair share of  gameplay conceits owing to its role as  an American installation during the Cold  War era is laced with bunkers and  nuclear silos each spanning miles  beneath the vibrant surface these  compounds foreshadow close quarters  combat yes along with side quests  involving hazardous nuclear material but  despite the danger they're often  littered with medical supplies and  weapon caches they present a risk reward  factor there yet another part of  breakpoints visual vocabulary that has  gameplay ramifications of its own less  common than the nuclear bunkers yet  useful in their own right  our Oeste jiz of world war ii these  range from the occasional foxhole to a  massive airfield to a crashed fighter  plane more often than not I've found  geographical Intel among the wreckage of  the Pacific Theater further aiding my  efforts to understand this massive  landmass it reinforces the sense of  people being here long before me with  knowledge I can still find useful even  at a time when murderous robots patrol  the shores that sense is amplified in  the next layer when I stumble upon one  of our OS abandoned villages or ancient  ruins while this dilapidated stratum  does make for great window dressing it  also houses some of my favorite side  missions involving the indigenous people  and the descendants who still farmed  fish and live off the land but even  before all this the Cold War the Second  Great War and the people who lived here  first is the oldest aspect of  breakpoints storytelling it's the  archipelago itself it's absolutely  packed with hidden caves and dense  rainforests it's many inlets and foggy  lagoons make for great  amphibious insertion points the terrain  is mountainous draining my energy as I  scramble up steep slopes and down eroded  cliff sides I can almost imagine the  tectonic shifts that created these  ridges the archipelago is the foundation  for Ubisoft Paris's world building and  it creeps into many of breakpoints  gameplay mechanics which is all to say  that breakpoints sense of time the idea  that this world has been around for ages  doesn't just stimulate my imagination it  plays an active role in how I approach  the game it encourages exploration and  rewards me for paying closer attention  somehow despite the fact that aura is a  fictional place and in turn has a  fictional history its past is informing  the present right in front of me a few  months ago during a visit to Ubisoft  Paris art director Benoit Martinez said  something that at the time seemed  innocuous he told me when you name  something you start to care about it  it's only now after 45 hours in Aurora  that I think I understand what he meant  Ghost Recon breakpoint is a severely  flawed game it suffers from sequel bloat  and it added progression systems that at  best are superfluous and at worse  actively detract from the game it's also  narrative ly rough and technically  jarring suffice it to say it has an  identity crisis but when I drop into  this world and take the time to admire  my surroundings I can't help but imagine  the effort it must have taken to give or  OA this sense of history I can't help  but love the way this history actually  effects the way I play the game despite  its frustrations and numerous  shortcomings breakpoint is a master  class in world building both in what we  can see and what we can't  [Music]  you
 

